\section{Preliminaries} 
Let $V = [n]=\{1,2,...,n\}$ denote the fixed set of vertices, where $[k]=\{1,2,..,k\}$ for any $k\in \mathbb{Z}^{>0}$. For any vertex $v\in V$, let $N(v)$ denote the set of neighbors of $v$ at any given point during the algorithm, and $d(v)=|N(v)|$ be the degree of $v$. A pair of vertices $(u,v)$ is called a \textit{non-edge} if $(u,v)\notin E$.

Let $\Delta$ denote the maximum degree of any vertex in the graph throughout the update sequence, which is known in advance. The coloring on $V$ maintained by our algorithm is denoted by $c:\,V\rightarrow [\Delta+1]$. Throughout the paper, we use the $\ti(\cdot)$ notation to hide a multiplicative $\text{polylog}(n)$ term. We say that an event $E$ happens \textit{with high probability} (abbreviated as w.h.p.) if $\Pr[E]\geq 1-\frac{1}{\text{poly}(n)}$. 

\section{A Fully Dynamic Sparse-Dense Decomposition}\label{sec: fullydynamicsparsedensedecomp}
In a sparse-dense decomposition, $V$ is partitioned into a set $V_{S}$ of \textit{sparse} vertices and a set $V_D$ of \textit{dense} vertices. The set $V_D$ is further partitioned into a collection of cliques $C_1, C_2, \ldots, C_k$, where for any $i\in [k]$, $C_i$ is an \textit{almost}-clique, that is, $|C_i|=\Theta(\Delta)$ and $|E(C_i)|\geq (1-\Omega(\varepsilon))\Delta^2$. The graph decomposition is parametrized by a small constant $\varepsilon>0$.

For a vertex $v$ in an almost-clique $C$, let $\b{E_C(v)}$ denote the total number of non-edges of $v$ in $C$, i.e. $\b{E_C(v)}=\{(u,v)| \, u\in C\backslash N(u)\}$. Let $\b{E(C)}=\bigcup_{v\in C} \b{E_C(v)}$ denote the set of all non-edges in $C$. 

\section{A Fully Dynamic \texorpdfstring{$(\Delta+1)$}{D+1}-Coloring Algorithm}\label{sec: fullydynamiccoloring}

\subsection{Working with a Fixed Decomposition for \texorpdfstring{$\Theta(\eps^2\Delta)$}{e2D} updates}\label{sec: constadjcomplexity} 
More precisely, we consider the sequence of updates partitioned into contiguous subsequences of $t$ updates each; each such subsequence is called a \textit{phase}.

